From 6fb5fb4050aba76ee3d02a20e90ecdb592451eff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Dvinskykh <david.dvinskykh@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 12:59:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(messages): inline buttons on send/edit and a callbacks
 action

Answering a queued decision took a typed message ("12 yes"); now it takes one
tap.

- message(action="send"|"edit") accepts `buttons`: rows of {text, data} mapped
  to the Bot API's inline_keyboard/callback_data. Validated before the request
  goes out (callback_data 1-64 bytes UTF-8, <=8 buttons per row, <=100 rows);
  on edit, buttons=[] strips the keyboard.
- message(action="callbacks") drains taps via getUpdates(allowed_updates=
  ["callback_query"]), answers each with answerCallbackQuery so the client's
  spinner stops, and optionally removes the keyboard (strip_buttons) so a
  question cannot be answered twice.
- The update cursor lives server-side (TELEGRAM_DATA_DIR/callback_cursor.json,
  keyed by bot id), so a repeated call never replays a tap the caller already
  acted on. since_id overrides it when the caller tracks its own position.
- TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_CALLBACK_SENDERS restricts who may tap the bot's buttons and
  TELEGRAM_CALLBACK_DATA_PATTERN validates the payload; rejected taps are
  logged, reported under "ignored", and never acknowledged.

Inline keyboards are bot-mode only -- the user backend raises ModeError for
buttons and for every callback method.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QEztxR4XvApFdJXxrX4mrM
